![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The admin bootstrapping logic is susceptible to a race condition. Since the `User_Roles` entry is created by an asynchronous database trigger (`on_auth_user_created`), it might not exist yet when this code runs, causing `roleData` to be null and the promotion to be skipped. Using an `upsert` operation with the admin client is more robust as it ensures the record is either created or updated to the `ADMIN` role regardless of the trigger's timing (provided the parent `Profiles` record exists).

```typescript
        // Use upsert to ensure the user is promoted to ADMIN, even if the trigger hasn't finished
        const adminSupabase = createAdminClient()
        await adminSupabase
          .from('User_Roles')
          .upsert({ 
            user_id: session.user.id, 
            role: 'ADMIN' 
          }, { onConflict: 'user_id' })
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Destructuring `user` directly from `data` can lead to a runtime error if the response is unexpected. It is safer to check for the `error` object or verify `data?.user` exists before proceeding. This ensures the application handles authentication failures gracefully.

```suggestion
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.getUser()
  const user = data?.user

  if (error || !user) {
    redirect('/')
  }
```
